I intended to do those 10 conducts from the beginning. Thanks to the
nice features of alt.org/nethack (specially the extended logfile list
of games), I can give an estimate for the number of tries it took for
this ascension: about 60 tries. There may be a couple of games in
those that were the other group of 10 conducts, and certainly there
were a lot of deaths due to reckless playing because of frustration of
previous game's ending. There were also some #quits (16) due do
breaking some conduct, and most of these happened because of prayer
before praying timeout (then again, to avoid this situation I prayed
lots of times only after fainting, which also gave a few deaths while
helpless). I also had another great run, which I mention on the 1st
post. That run was also powered by some early bones =SD, and it ended
on orcus town, while I stupidly zapped an un-identified wand to try to
revive my pet (it was polymorph). This was only my 2nd run, so if I
had not did that stupid mistake I would have ended with an
unrealistic 50% ratio.

This rant serves to give an idea of the early game difficulties, and
the unlikeness of someone doing a game like this if not aiming to it.

> > On dlvl 6 (oracle) I found a great bones from EricHerman. [...]
>
> I counted four bones levels, and there were quite some nice items in
> the bones. Maybe hard to tell, but how do you think would this hard
> conduct game have evolved if you hadn't found those; what would your
> strategy in this case have been?

On my post I give each bones attention proportional to their
usefulness. The 1st bones was really important, but I could have done
it without the others (I didn't even touch the Juiblex). This game in
particular would have ended pretty soon without that bones. IIRC there
was no other =SD on the entire dungeon, and as you can see by the
starting rant that is fundamental. I did have at least one good
bones-less-=SD run. I had found a random =SD and identified it with a
sink. I think I also had found some goodies, but I was lacking =PC and
[MR, so I was doing the mines (for the luckstone) very carefully,
searching several times on every square. On some level I suspected
there was a poly trap, so I was searching 5 or 6 times on every
square, and I also had intrinsic search. Even so I did not find the
trap that ruined that run. I felt robbed, and when I found this game
bones I considered it a rng payment with interest. But yes, these
games require an unlikely combination of loot found, namely =SD,
reflection, [oMR (or at least =PC, or you can get MR from the eyes).

As for strategy, the only (more or less) fixed part is getting an
early =SD: get some rings (kick sinks, do sokoban, rob stores) and
drop them on sinks.


